Subject: tests: Run the cross-reply exactness test under PC24 with TEST_PC24=1.

Requires 0004. Since upstream 052944f (#57), with native slots an fild m64
whose value is only stored with fistp m64 or left on the stack stays a
LoadF64 that holds the integer's bits (kExactI64 when it is on the stack),
and the run's end writes its exact 80-bit value back into the slot. 0004
accepts LoadF64 in a single-precision PC24 body: the body narrows those bits
as if they were a binary64 value, exactly or by leaving for the exact body,
and widens them back before the shared write-back. That keeps them bit for
bit, but upstream's test never sets the control word, so it never reaches a
PC24 body.

TEST_PC24=1 sets control word 0x007f (TMNF's PC24, round to nearest) once
before the first case, as the PC24 switches of 0001's tests do. Unset, the
test is unchanged.

diff --git a/tests/test_x87_cross_reply_exact.c b/tests/test_x87_cross_reply_exact.c
index 3d4f63c..90f29e5 100644
--- a/tests/test_x87_cross_reply_exact.c
+++ b/tests/test_x87_cross_reply_exact.c
@@ -40,9 +40,14 @@
  *
  * Expected: passes under stock Rosetta and under x87sidecar, fails with
  * X87_NATIVE_SLOTS=0 (run_tests.sh checks both).
+ *
+ * TEST_PC24=1 runs every case with control word 0x007f (PC24, round to
+ * nearest), set once in its own block before the first case, so the IR runs
+ * of the last group also take the single-precision PC24 bodies.
  */
 #include <stdint.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
 
 static int failures = 0;
@@ -543,6 +548,10 @@ int main(int argc, char** argv) {
         dump |= strcmp(argv[i], "--dump") == 0;
         int_stores_may_round |= strcmp(argv[i], "--int-stores-may-round") == 0;
     }
+    if (getenv("TEST_PC24")) {
+        const uint16_t cw = 0x007f;
+        __asm__ volatile("fldcw %0" : : "m"(cw));
+    }
     int cases = 0;
     for (size_t mi = 0; mi < sizeof(kMiddles) / sizeof(kMiddles[0]); mi++) {
         const middle* m = &kMiddles[mi];
